{"tool_name":"mirtrace","contents":["biocontainers","galaxy","bioschemas","biotools","bioconda","oeb"],"fetched_metadata":{"bioconda":{"name":"mirtrace","version":"1.0.1","home":"https://github.com/friedlanderlab/mirtrace","license":"GPL-2.0","summary":"miRTrace is a new quality control and taxonomic tracing tool developed specifically for small RNA sequencing data (sRNA-Seq). \nEach sample is characterized by profiling sequencing quality, read length, sequencing depth and miRNA complexity and also the \namounts of miRNAs versus undesirable sequences (derived from tRNAs, rRNAs and sequencing artifacts). In addition to these routine \nquality control (QC) analyses, miRTrace can accurately and sensitively resolve taxonomic origins of small RNA-Seq data based on the \ncomposition of clade-specific miRNAs. This feature can be used to detect cross-clade contaminations in typical lab settings. It can \nalso be applied for more specific applications in forensics, food quality control and clinical diagnosis, for instance tracing the \norigins of meat products or detecting parasitic microRNAs in host serum.\n","identifiers":["biotools:mirtrace","doi:10.1186/s13059-018-1588-9"],"build":{"noarch":"generic","number":1},"requirements":{"host":null,"run":["openjdk >=8.0.144","python"]},"source_url":"https://github.com/friedlanderlab/mirtrace/releases/download/v1.0.1/mirtrace-v1.0.1.zip","test":{"commands":["mirtrace -v"]}},"biocontainers":{"name":"mirtrace","identifiers":["biotools:mirtrace","doi:10.1186/s13059-018-1588-9"],"license":"GPL-2.0","summary":"Mirtrace is a new quality control and taxonomic tracing tool developed specifically for small rna sequencing data (srna-seq). each sample is characterized by profiling sequencing quality, read length, sequencing depth and mirna complexity and also the \\namounts of mirnas versus undesirable sequences (derived from trnas, rrnas and sequencing artifacts). in addition to these routine quality control (qc) analyses, mirtrace can accurately and sensitively resolve taxonomic origins of small rna-seq data based on the composition of clade-specific mirnas. this feature can be used to detect cross-clade contaminations in typical lab settings. it can also be applied for more specific applications in forensics, food quality control and clinical diagnosis, for instance tracing the origins of meat products or detecting parasitic micrornas in host serum.","home_url":"https://github.com/friedlanderlab/mirtrace","keywords":["Animals","Humans","MicroRNAs","Molecular Typing","Sequence Analysis, RNA","Functional, regulatory and non-coding RNA","RNA-seq","Parasitology","RNA-Seq"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":0},"biotools":{"id":"miRTrace","home":"https://github.com/friedlanderlab/mirtrace","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Tool for quality control and tracing taxonomic origins of microRNA sequencing data.","addition_date":"2019-08-11T17:32:06Z","last_update_date":"2020-06-16T10:55:25Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:miRTrace","cost":"Free of charge","documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://github.com/friedlanderlab/mirtrace/blob/master/README.md"},{"type":["User manual"],"url":"https://github.com/friedlanderlab/mirtrace/blob/master/release-bundle-includes/manual.pdf"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Sequence trimming","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3192"},{"term":"miRNA expression analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3792"},{"term":"miRNA target prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0463"}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"miRTrace","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"Ruta","publication":[{"doi":"10.1186/s13059-018-1588-9","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2018 The Author(s).We present here miRTrace, the first algorithm to trace microRNA sequencing data back to their taxonomic origins. This is a challenge with profound implications for forensics, parasitology, food control, and research settings where cross-contamination can compromise results. miRTrace accurately (> 99%) assigns real and simulated data to 14 important animal and plant groups, sensitively detects parasitic infection in mammals, and discovers the primate origin of single cells. Applying our algorithm to over 700 public datasets, we find evidence that over 7% are cross-contaminated and present a novel solution to clean these computationally, even after sequencing has occurred. miRTrace is freely available at https://github.com/friedlanderlab/mirtrace.","authors":[{"name":"Biryukova I."},{"name":"Eldfjell Y."},{"name":"Estivill X."},{"name":"Friedlander M.R."},{"name":"Fromm B."},{"name":"Kang W."}],"citationCount":11,"date":"2018-12-04T00:00:00Z","journal":"Genome Biology","title":"MiRTrace reveals the organismal origins of microRNA sequencing data"},"pmcid":"PMC6280396","pmid":"30514392","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Functional, regulatory and non-coding RNA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0659"},{"term":"Parasitology","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3302"},{"term":"RNA-seq","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3170"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"miRTrace","home":"https://bio.tools/miRTrace","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Tool for quality control and tracing taxonomic origins of microRNA sequencing data.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"galaxy":{"version":"1.0.1","first_commit":"2026-07-21","conda_name":"mirtrace","conda_version":"1.0.1","summary":"miRTrace is a tool for quality control and tracing of small RNA sequencing data. It profiles sequencing quality, read length, sequencing depth and miRNA complexity, the amounts of miRNAs versus undesirable sequences (derived from tRNAs, rRNAs and sequencing artifacts) and the composition of clade-specific miRNAs.","edam_operations":["Sequence trimming","miRNA target prediction","miRNA expression analysis"],"edam_topics":["Functional, regulatory and non-coding RN